      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true,
        "id": "iLRFI8RkQqvb"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Sensitivity of the Calibrated Yield Curve to Methodology\n",
        "\n",
        "The calibrated yield curve is **highly sensitive** to the chosen methodology.\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Time-Series Calibration (MLE / Historical):** Calibrating parameters ($\\kappa$, $\\theta$, $\\sigma$) using historical time-series data reflects the *real-world physical measure* ($\\mathbb{P}$). This is effective for forecasting the future trajectory of the short rate, but it is fundamentally incorrect for pricing bonds today, resulting in poor or negative $R^2$ scores when reconstructing the current curve.\n",
        "* **Cross-Sectional Calibration (Risk-Neutral):** Transitioning to a cross-sectional approach (e.g., using the Nelder-Mead algorithm) calibrates the model to the *Risk-Neutral measure* ($\\mathbb{Q}$). By minimizing the Mean Squared Error across all current maturities rather than looking at past history, the model accurately prices the curve, driving the out-of-sample $R^2$ up to **0.8759**.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Conclusion:** Time-series calibration builds a predictive forecasting tool; cross-sectional calibration builds an accurate pricing tool."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true,
        "id": "L3OxsybIQqvb"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Mean-Reversion Speed ($\\kappa$) and Shock Persistence\n",
        "\n",
        "The mean-reversion speed ($\\kappa$) acts as the \"gravitational pull\" drawing the current interest rate ($r_t$) back to the long-term average ($\\theta$). It directly dictates the **half-life** of an interest rate shock, defined mathematically as $t_{1/2} = \\ln(2) / \\kappa$.\n",
        "\n",
        "* **High $\\kappa$ (Fast Reversion):** Implies a highly reactive central bank. Shocks are transient and aggressively steered back to normal.\n",
        "* **Low $\\kappa$ (Slow Reversion):** Implies a sluggish policy response. Shocks become persistent and impact the yield curve for a long duration.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Data Implication:**\n",
        "In the final Risk-Neutral optimization, the model calibrated an optimal $\\kappa^*$ of **0.2537**. Using the half-life formula ($\\ln(2) / 0.2537$), this implies that an unexpected macroeconomic shock will take approximately **2.73 years** to dissipate by half. This indicates a market regime where shocks have moderate-to-long persistence, reflecting a relatively sticky interest rate environment."
      ]
    },
