        "### Systematic Errors in the Base CIR Model\n",
        "\n",
        "**Where it over/underestimates:**\n",
        "The Base CIR model (calibrated historically via MLE) systematically **underestimated yields during periods of high volatility or sudden macroeconomic shocks**. When attempting to predict out-of-sample paths, the Base CIR yielded massive percentage errors (a MAPE of ~74.9%).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why this happens:**\n",
        "This systematic error stems from the model's reliance on standard Brownian motion ($dW_t$). Brownian motion simulates continuous, normally distributed increments, completely failing to account for the \"fat tails\" present in real-world financial data. When a central bank makes a sudden policy shift, the market experiences a discontinuous jump. The Base CIR is mathematically constrained to \"walk\" to that new rate smoothly, causing it to systematically underestimate the magnitude and speed of actual yield spikes during the test period."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "sNB2jtJ2Qqve"
      },
      "source": [
        "# Section D : Model Improvement & Extensions\n",
        "## Jump-Diffusion Processes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0i8q7gnbQqve"
      },
      "source": [
        "The base Cox-Ingersoll-Ross (CIR) model relies on continuous Brownian motion ($dW_t$) to simulate interest rate paths. While this effectively captures general mean-reverting behavior, it structurally assumes that interest rates move in smooth, continuous increments. In reality, financial markets exhibit \"fat tails\" and sudden, discontinuous jumps—often triggered by unexpected macroeconomic shocks, sudden central bank policy announcements, or systemic crises.\n",
        "\n",
        "To address this limitation and fulfill the advanced extension requirement, we will implement a **Jump-Diffusion Process**, building upon the framework established by Duffie, Pan, and Singleton (2000).\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Mathematical Framework\n",
        "We extend the standard CIR Stochastic Differential Equation (SDE) by incorporating a compound Poisson jump process. The modified SDE is:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$dr_t = \\kappa(\\theta - r_t)dt + \\sigma\\sqrt{r_t}dW_t + J_t dN_t$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Where:\n",
        "* **$N_t$**: A Poisson process dictating the arrival of jumps, governed by an intensity parameter $\\lambda_{jump}$ (the expected number of jumps per year).\n",
        "* **$J_t$**: The random magnitude of the jump when it occurs, drawn from a defined probability distribution (e.g., Normal or Log-Normal).\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Implementation & Calibration Strategy\n",
        "Rather than using arbitrary parameters, we will ground our model in empirical data. The following code cell will:\n",
        "1. **Extract Jumps:** Analyze the daily residuals of our training data to isolate extreme market movements (defined as $>3\\sigma$ outliers).\n",
        "2. **Calibrate Parameters:** Calculate the historical jump intensity ($\\lambda_{jump}$) and jump volatility ($\\sigma_{jump}$) based on these localized outliers.\n",
        "3. **Simulate & Compare:** Run Monte Carlo simulations of both the Base CIR and the new Jump-Diffusion CIR, evaluating which model better captures the variance of the out-of-sample test yields."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true,
        "id": "cKeTi6lIQqve"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 1. Mathematical Justification for the Jump-Diffusion Extension\n",
        "\n",
        "**The Structure of the Choice:**\n",
        "The choice to implement a Jump-Diffusion process over a Two-Factor or Time-Dependent (CIR++) model is justified by the fundamental mathematical limitations of continuous diffusions. The base CIR SDE relies on standard Brownian motion ($dW_t$), which forces interest rate paths to be continuous. This structurally assumes that rates move in smooth, infinitesimal increments.\n",
        "\n",
        "By adding a compound Poisson process ($J_t dN_t$), we break the continuity assumption. The mathematical structure $dr_t = \\kappa(\\theta - r_t)dt + \\sigma\\sqrt{r_t}dW_t + J_t dN_t$ explicitly separates \"normal\" daily market noise (handled by the diffusion term $\\sigma\\sqrt{r_t}dW_t$) from \"abnormal\" macroeconomic shocks (handled by the jump term).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why not the alternatives?**\n",
        "While a Two-Factor CIR model is excellent for explaining the *shape* of the yield curve (separating level and slope), it still relies on continuous diffusions and fails to simulate sudden discontinuities. Similarly, the Time-Dependent CIR (CIR++) model fits the initial curve perfectly by making parameters deterministic functions of time, but it does not organically simulate unexpected, random future shocks. Jump-Diffusion was the mathematically superior choice for capturing the non-Gaussian, heavy-tailed distributions actually observed in your interest rate data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 19,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "QLgu_e5MQqve",
        "outputId": "0bb00cca-fdcb-4ad0-978f-ccbd4899b28b"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "CIR Jump-Diffusion Model class initialized.\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "class CIRJumpDiffusionModel:\n",
        "    def __init__(self, kappa, theta, sigma, lambd=0.0, jump_intensity=0.0, jump_mean=0.0, jump_std=0.0):\n",
        "        self.kappa = kappa\n",
        "        self.theta = theta\n",
        "        self.sigma = sigma\n",
        "        self.lambd = lambd\n",
        "        # Jump parameters\n",
        "        self.jump_intensity = jump_intensity # lambda_jump: Expected jumps per year\n",
        "        self.jump_mean = jump_mean           # mu_jump: Mean jump size\n",
        "        self.jump_std = jump_std             # sigma_jump: Volatility of the jump\n",
        "\n",
        "    def simulate_path(self, r0, dt, n_steps, seed=None):\n",
        "        if seed is not None:\n",
        "            np.random.seed(seed)\n",
        "\n",
        "        r = np.zeros(n_steps + 1)\n",
        "        r[0] = r0\n",
        "\n",
        "        for i in range(n_steps):\n",
        "            dW = np.random.normal(0, np.sqrt(dt))\n",
        "\n",
        "            # Simulate Poisson jump occurrences in interval dt\n",
        "            dn = np.random.poisson(self.jump_intensity * dt)\n",
        "\n",
        "            # Calculate total jump size if a jump occurs\n",
        "            jump_size = 0.0\n",
        "            if dn > 0:\n",
        "                jump_size = np.sum(np.random.normal(self.jump_mean, self.jump_std, dn))\n",
        "\n",
        "            # Base CIR Euler-Maruyama step + Jump component\n",
        "            r[i + 1] = (\n",
        "                r[i]\n",
        "                + self.kappa * (self.theta - r[i]) * dt\n",
        "                + self.sigma * np.sqrt(max(r[i], 1e-8)) * dW\n",
        "                + jump_size\n",
        "            )\n",
        "            # Enforce strict positivity\n",
        "            r[i + 1] = max(r[i + 1], 1e-8)\n",
        "\n",
        "        return r\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"CIR Jump-Diffusion Model class initialized.\")"
      ]
    },
