        "###  Qualitative Impact of Jump Processes on Yield Curves During Stress\n",
        "\n",
        "**Discontinuous Shifts vs. High Variance:**\n",
        "In a standard one-factor CIR model, the only way to simulate a \"stress period\" is to artificially inflate the volatility parameter ($\\sigma$). This causes the predicted future distribution of rates to symmetrically widen over time, but the paths themselves remain continuous.\n",
        "\n",
        "When a jump process is introduced, a stress event triggers an immediate, discrete shift in the short rate ($r_t$). Qualitatively, this forces the predicted yield curve to experience an instant parallel (or semi-parallel) shift upward or downward.\n",
        "\n",
        "**The Reversion Hump:**\n",
        "More importantly, because the jump shifts $r_t$ violently away from the long-term mean ($\\theta$), the mean-reversion component ($\\kappa(\\theta - r_t)$) immediately exerts maximum gravitational pull. During a stress period, the jump-diffusion model will often produce a yield curve with a pronounced \"hump\" or steep inversion—reflecting the market's expectation that while the short rate has spiked due to a crisis, the central bank will eventually drag it back to the equilibrium rate $\\theta$ over the horizon dictated by $\\kappa$."
      ]
    },
